  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 120 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nFriday through Wednesday:\n\nA positively-tilted 500mb trough tracks southeastward across the \nmidwest late this week, with a corresponding surface low developing \nnear Nebraska / the Dakotas late Thursday and crossing Illinois \nFriday night into Saturday morning. The track of this system (south \nof our region) should strongly limit the buildup of instability, \nwith most guidance suggesting ~500 joules or less MUCAPE. As such, \nwe're expecting showers and weak thunderstorms to gradually \noverspread southern WI from west to east on Friday (65-85%\nchances). Shower and weak storm chances continue into Friday\nnight and taper down into Saturday. Though overall precip\nchances are relatively high with this event, a known bias in\nlong-range model guidance is to over emphasize the synoptic\nfeatures (ex. the precip shield on the north edge of the low)\nwith the forecast QPF then shifting south towards the\ninstability axis / deep convection when high-res model guidance\nenters the picture. As such, it's too early to \"guarantee\" \nrainfall across all of southern WI at this time, but the odds\nare high for at least some of the region to receive some much-\nneeded rainfall. Our highest confidence in precip is over far\nsouthwestern WI. \n\nPredominantly dry weather expected Sunday into Monday as high \npressure settles into the region. A slow warming trend unfolds early \nnext week as the high continues southeastward. The upper ridge axis \npasses overhead Tuesday, suggesting that the warmth (perhaps mid to \nupper 80s high temps) may peak then. Predominantly zonal flow \nlooking likely in the jet through next week, though a few ripples \nmerit some slight chances for showers and thunderstorms in the \nextended forecast.\n\nSheppard",
